00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.380
Jack: How is data stored?

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:06.080
Data storage allows data to be stored
on a computer when it powers down.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.110
All data in a computer is stored as a
number, but not a conventional number.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.720
Conventional numbers use ten
different digits but binary

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.730
numbers use two digits only.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.919
Data is converted into binary digital form
using two digits 1 and 0 to be stored.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.060
A bit is the smallest unit of
data representing a single value.

00:00:30.639 --> 00:00:33.080
A byte is eight binary digits long.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.989
Leah: The conventional numbers
0-8 translate into 0, 1, 10,

00:00:39.209 --> 00:00:48.850
11, 100, 101, 110, 111 and 1000.

00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:55.100
So when data is stored even letters become
numbers, for example the words in a book

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.930
would be stored one byte per letter.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.850
Photographs are also converted to
numbers that indicate the location,

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.770
colour and brightness of each pixel.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.000
Jack: Storage and memory is often
measured in megabytes and gigabytes and

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.760
even terabytes, however, as units of
data measurement begin to grow so does

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.830
the amount of data collected and stored.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:21.270
Binary data is primarily stored
on the hard disk drive [HDD].

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:27.249
In addition to this storage can be
on SSD flash drives and memory cards.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.140
With this type of storage, the
binary numbers are stored as

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.560
a series of electrical charges
within NAND flash chips.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.809
Leah: To store data regardless of its
form, users, need to use storage devices.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:45.510
These will be either 'direct area'
storage or 'network based' storage.

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:50.079
Direct area storage is directly
attached to a computer.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:54.590
It can offer back up services or can be
used as storage in conjunction with a

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.850
program that may be memory intensive.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.929
Network based storage allows more
than one computer to access it

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.650
through the network making it better
for sharing and collaborating.

00:02:06.129 --> 00:02:08.660
It may be off site or even cloud based.

